-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/buyers/closing-costs/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How much are closing costs in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Buyers typically pay roughly 2 to 5 percent of the price in closing costs and prepaids, covering lender fees, the attorney, title insurance, and escrow deposits. Benchmark surveys put the third-party-plus-tax portion near $2,900 on an average South Carolina purchase, before lender fees and prepaids. Sellers pay the commission they negotiate plus about 0.37 percent in deed stamps and small document fees."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who pays the transfer tax in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The seller, by statute. South Carolina's deed recording fee is $1.85 per $500 of price, which is $1,480 on a $400,000 sale, and state law makes it the grantor's liability. It is often called deed stamps or transfer tax; the money splits $1.30 to the state and $0.55 to the county."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need an attorney to close on a house in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The South Carolina Supreme Court requires a licensed attorney to supervise real estate closings, including the title work, document preparation, recording, and disbursement. Residential closing fees typically run $600 to $1,200, and the buyer customarily selects and pays the closing attorney."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a CL-100 letter?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"South Carolina's official wood infestation report, covering termites and wood-destroying organisms. Most lenders require one, it typically costs about $75 to $150, and it is ordered near closing because it is valid for roughly 45 days. Homes with crawl spaces are the ones to watch."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why is my Horry County tax escrow so high?","acceptedAnswer":{"