+<main id="main"><div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/invest/">Invest</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Growth, roads and permits</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">2026 election</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Did the 2026 election change building rules in Horry County?<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)"></em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Chief Operating Officer, and <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Abdulla Hijazi</strong>, Chief Marketing Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; <span style="white-space:nowrap">Updated November 9, 2026</span></p><p class="detail-sub">The 2026 election changed no rule for building in Horry County. The road tax, the impact fees and the flood rules are the same as before the vote.</p></div></div><section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The short answer</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Voters approved the road money, the RIDE IV sales tax, in 2024. The county council approved the current impact fee schedule before the election, and no road or growth question was on the Horry ballot.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Interstate 73 has no start date, because funding for most of its $1.6 billion cost has not been identified. The leaders who won have road plans, and none of those plans is a law yet.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;ma