2" role="img" font-family="system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Roboto,sans-serif" aria-label="Column chart of single-family homes permitted in Horry County each year from 2005 to 2025. 6,484 in 2006, down to 1,388 in 2010, then up most years to 7,269 in 2024, the most of any year shown, and 6,418 in 2025."><line x1="48" x2="680" y1="434.0" y2="434.0" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".35"/><text x="43" y="438.9" text-anchor="end" font-size="14" fill="#4a4f5c">0</text><line x1="48" x2="680" y1="333.2" y2="333.2" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".1"/><text x="43" y="338.1" text-anchor="end" font-size="14" fill="#4a4f5c">2,000</text><line x1="48" x2="680" y1="232.4" y2="232.4" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".1"/><text x="43" y="237.3" text-anchor="end" font-size="14" fill="#4a4f5c">4,000</text><line x1="48" x2="680" y1="131.6" y2="131.6" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".1"/><text x="43" y="136.5" text-anchor="end" font-size="14" fill="#4a4f5c">6,000</text><line x1="48" x2="680" y1="30.8" y2="30.8" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".1"/><text x="43" y="35.7" text-anchor="end" font-size="14" fill="#4a4f5c">8,000</text><rect x="52.8" y="107.9" width="20.5" height="326.1" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="82.9" y="107.2" width="20.5" height="326.8" rx="3.0" fill="#c4783a" fill-opacity="1"/><text x="93.1" y="100.9" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">6,484</text><rect x="113.0" y="241.8" width="20.5" height="192.2" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="143.1" y="339.9" width="20.5" height="94.1" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="173.2" y="360.6" width="20.5" height="73.4" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="203.3" y="364.0" width="20.5" height="70.0" rx="3.0" fill="#c4783a" fill-opacity="1"/><text x="213.5" y="357.7" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">1,388</text><rect x="233.4" y="359.4" width="20.5" height="74.6" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="263.5" y="331.7" width="20.5" height="102.3" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="293.6" y="302.7" width="20.5" height="131.3" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="323.7" y="279.5" width="20.5" height="154.5" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="353.8" y="253.7" width="20.5" height="180.3" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="383.9" y="241.0" width="20.5" height="193.0" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="414.0" y="199.3" width="20.5" height="234.7" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="444.1" y="225.9" width="20.5" height="208.1" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="474.1" y="212.8" width="20.5" height="221.2" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="504.2" y="181.6" width="20.5" height="252.4" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="534.3" y="95.1" width="20.5" height="338.9" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="564.4" y="84.1" width="20.5" height="349.9" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="594.5" y="113.7" width="20.5" height="320.3" rx="3.0" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".45"/><rect x="624.6" y="67.6" width="20.5" height="366.4" rx="3.0" fill="#c4783a" fill-opacity="1"/><text x="634.9" y="61.3" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">7,269</text><rect x="654.7" y="110.5" width="20.5" height="323.5" rx="3.0" fill="#c4783a" fill-opacity="1"/><text x="63.0" y="456.4" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="#4a4f5c">2005</text><text x="213.5" y="456.4" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="#4a4f5c">2010</text><text x="364.0" y="456.4" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="#4a4f5c">2015</text><text x="514.5" y="456.4" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="#4a4f5c">2020</text><text x="665.0" y="456.4" text-anchor="middle" font-size="14" fill="#4a4f5c">2025</text></svg><figcaption>From 6,484 in 2006 to 1,388 in 2010, then up in most years to the most of any year shown, 7,269 in 2024. Census county permit counts.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the last housing slowdown, permits dropped by about four in five from 2006 to 2010, and the county did not pass 6,000 again until 2021.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Almost all of today's building is single-family homes. Units in buildings of five or more, such as apartments and condos, were 5,132 in 2005 and 77 in the latest year. For the monthly count and the communities being built now, see <a href="/invest/what-is-being-built/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">what is being built on the Grand Strand</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="who-won"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Who won">What did the 2026 election change for building in Horry County?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Every county council seat on the ballot went to a Republican, 4 of the 7 without an opponent.</p><div class="g7-tab"><table><caption>Who won in the unofficial count, and what each office can change for building here</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Office</th><th scope="col">Who won</th><th scope="col">What it can change</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Horry County Council</th><td><span class="l">Who won</span>Chair Johnny Gardner, re-elected unopposed. District seats, all Republican: 1 Jenna Dukes, 2 Bill Howard, 5 Tyler Servant, 7 Tom Anderson, 8 Michael "Mash" Masciarelli, 11 Al Allen. County results are uncertified.</td><td><span class="l">What it can change</span>Impact fees, zoning and the county's flood building rules. State law <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t06c029.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">allows counties and cities to zone</a>.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Governor</th><td><span class="l">Who won</span>Alan Wilson (R), 51.71%. Jermaine Johnson (D) conceded.</td><td><span class="l">What it can change</span>Proposes the state's executive budget, where money for <span class="nw">I-73</span> could be asked for.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">S.C. House, 124 seats</th><td><span class="l">Who won</span>Republicans 79, Democrats 45, down from 89 Republicans and 35 Democrats. State Senate seats were not on the ballot, except one special race in District 15.</td><td><span class="l">What it can change</span>State law, including the law that allows impact fees.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">U.S. Senate</th><td><span class="l">Who won</span>Annie Andrews (D), 51.02%. The AP called the race.</td><td><span class="l">What it can change</span>Federal law and spending, with the House.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">U.S. House, 7th District</th><td><span class="l">Who won</span>Russell Fry (R), 56.75%, re-elected. The AP called the race.</td><td><span class="l">What it can change</span>Federal law and spending, for the district that includes Horry County.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="c3-aerial c3-aerial-close"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-e/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/horry-county-government-complex-conway-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-e/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/horry-county-government-complex-conway-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-e/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/horry-county-government-complex-conway-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="800" alt="The Horry County courthouse and county office complex in Conway, two red brick buildings joined by a white entrance" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Pollinator. License: CC BY-SA 3.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 3.0">Photo: Pollinator</a></span></div><figcaption>The Horry County courthouse and county office complex in Conway, the county seat.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">CBS News projects that Democrats will control the U.S. Senate in January. Statewide results stay unofficial until the state certifies them. Every race is on <a href="/market-reports/2026-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our page on what the 2026 election means for Myrtle Beach buyers and sellers</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="ride-iv"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="RIDE IV">What is RIDE IV, the Horry County road tax?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">RIDE IV is a 1% sales tax for road projects in Horry County, approved by voters in November 2024.</p><div class="g7-stats" role="list" aria-label="RIDE IV in three numbers"><div role="listitem"><b>56.78%</b>of Horry voters said yes in 2024</div><div role="listitem"><b>25 years</b>or $6.35 billion, whichever comes first</div><div role="listitem"><b>$450 million</b>for <span class="nw">I-73</span>, as WIS reported the list</div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One item is a local match for <span class="nw">I-73</span>: up to half the cost of building it from <span class="nw">SC 22</span> to the Horry County line. That money can be spent only after SCDOT, the state Department of Transportation, has signed contracts to finish <span class="nw">I-73</span> from Horry County to <span class="nw">I-95</span>. The <a href="https://www.wistv.com/2026/08/26/i-73-project-gains-renewed-attention-after-trump-pledge/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WIS report</a> on <span class="nw">I-73</span> gives the $450 million figure.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="i-73"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="I-73">When will I-73 be built in South Carolina?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No start date has been announced. Work cannot begin until SCDOT identifies where the money comes from. The South Carolina section is estimated at $1.6 billion, and funding for most of that cost has not been identified.</p><div class="g7-tab"><table><caption><span class="nw">I-73</span> in South Carolina: what has happened</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">When</th><th scope="col">What happened</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">November 2024</th><td><span class="l">What happened</span>Horry voters approved RIDE IV. Its <span class="nw">I-73</span> match can be spent only once SCDOT has signed contracts to finish the road to <span class="nw">I-95</span>.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2024 to 2026</th><td><span class="l">What happened</span>Gov. Henry McMaster left <span class="nw">I-73</span> out of his executive budget in each of the three years.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">February 2026</th><td><span class="l">What happened</span>SCDOT reported the <a href="https://www.wmbfnews.com/2026/02/10/scdot-makes-progress-right-of-way-acquisition-proposed-i-73/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">right of way</a> 99% bought between <span class="nw">I-95</span> and <span class="nw">US 501</span>, and 95% between <span class="nw">US 501</span> and the Little Pee Dee River.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">August 2026</th><td><span class="l">What happened</span>President Trump said "they're going to have the funding that's necessary." He named no source for the money.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What has to happen before building starts</h3><ol class="g7-steps" aria-label="I-73, what has to happen next"><li class="next"><strong>The money</strong> Funding for the rest of the $1.6 billion has to be identified.</li><li class="next"><strong>The contracts</strong> SCDOT signs the contracts to finish <span class="nw">I-73</span> to <span class="nw">I-95</span>. After that, the RIDE IV match can be spent.</li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Governor-elect Wilson will propose the next executive budgets. Neither leader's plan names a road project.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="plans"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Leaders' plans">What do the new leaders say about roads, growth and federal money?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each plan below is in the leader's own words, from a campaign page or an interview. Each one needs other officials to act before anything changes in Horry County.</p><div class="g7-grid"><div class="g7-card"><h3>Annie Andrews (D), U.S. Senate</h3><dl><dt>Roads</dt><dd>"Fix and modernize South Carolina's roads, bridges, and highways," and "make sure federal dollars actually reach our communities," from her <a href="https://drannieandrews.com/issue/transportation-infrastructure-that-works/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">infrastructure page</a>.</dd><dt>Building</dt><dd>She would "provide tax cuts to homebuilders to incentivize more construction" and end "chaotic and unilateral tariffs." Her <a href="https://drannieandrews.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Web-file.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">plan</a> names home building materials among the goods tariffs make costlier.</dd><dt>Permits</dt><dd>She backs cutting "red tape that makes it harder to build new energy infrastructure."</dd><dt>Growth</dt><dd>A two-year national moratorium on building AI data centers.</dd><dt>Who must act</dt><dd>Both houses of Congress pass a bill, and the president signs it.</dd></dl></div><div class="g7-card"><h3>Alan Wilson (R), governor</h3><dl><dt>Roads</dt><dd>"We need to cut down the time it takes to build a road or a bridge in South Carolina." His <a href="https://wilsonforsc.com/priorities/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">priorities</a> say he will "modernize the Department of Transportation" and "streamline project timelines."</dd><dt>Building</dt><dd>"Make it easier for homes to be built, which lowers the cost of those homes." He tied home costs to <a href="https://www.wistv.com/2026/10/28/full-interview-alan-wilson-shares-priorities-governor-ahead-election-day/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">waiting on permits</a>.</dd><dt>Growth</dt><dd>Independent performance audits of state agencies, overseen by the lieutenant governor-elect, Mike Reichenbach.</dd><dt>Who must act</dt><dd>The governor's office for the audits. A change in state law needs the General Assembly. Home permits are issued by counties and cities.</dd></dl></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Rep. Russell Fry (R) was re-elected in the 7th District, which includes Horry County. Any federal road money for <span class="nw">I-73</span> would need votes in both the House and the Senate.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="impact-fees"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Impact fees">What are Horry County impact fees on a new home?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A single-family home of about 1,500 square feet pays $1,615 in total in Horry County impact fees. The fee depends on the kind of building and its size. The county council approved this schedule unanimously in July 2026, as Ordinance 30-2026. Horry County has charged impact fees since 2021.</p><div class="g7-stats g7-one" role="list" aria-label="Horry County impact fee for a typical home"><div role="listitem"><b>$1,615</b>for a single-family home of about 1,500 square feet</div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Impact fees are a county power under the state's <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t06c001.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Development Impact Fee Act</a>. Before charging them on homes, a county must estimate the effect on affordable housing, and the ordinance needs a positive majority of the council. Any change to the fee is a vote of the county council, including the members elected this year.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On a new home, we ask the builder whether the fee is already in the price, so you compare homes on the same terms. Our <a href="/buyers/new-construction/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">guide to buying new construction</a> covers the rest of the process.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="flood-rules"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Flood rules">What are the flood building rules for a new home in Horry County?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In a flood zone, the lowest floor of a new home must be above the expected level of a big flood. The extra height is called freeboard. In the unincorporated county <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/emergency-management/flooding/build-responsibly/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the rule is 3 feet above that flood level in AE and VE zones</a>. In an AE zone that is the lowest floor. In a VE zone it is the bottom of the lowest supporting beam. A surveyor's certificate on the finished building proves the height.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In an A zone with no flood level on the map, the lowest floor must be at least 24 inches above the highest ground next to the building. The building inspector checks it. The county council <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/3rxjkrew/horry-county-resiliency-report-summary_07oct22.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">raised the freeboard from 1 foot to 3 feet when it rewrote its flood ordinance in summer 2021</a>. The rule is now <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/stormwater/engineers/resources-tools/ordinances-manuals-and-reports/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chapter 9 of the county code</a>. Cities have their own rules. For a lot inside a city, ask the city.</p><figure class="g7-fig"><span class="cap">Floor height above the flood level for a new home in a flood zone</span><svg class="g7-fb" viewBox="0 0 340 200" role="img" font-family="system-ui,-apple-system,Segoe UI,Roboto,sans-serif" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;max-width:420px;margin:0 auto" aria-label="Drawing of two houses on posts in a flood zone. The left house's floor is 1 foot above the flood level. The right house's floor is 3 feet above it, the height the county requires now."><rect x="0" y="150" width="340" height="50" fill="#5c8fb0" fill-opacity=".28"/><line x1="0" x2="340" y1="150" y2="150" stroke="#2c5d80" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 4"/><text x="170" y="170" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill="#1c2028">Flood level</text><rect x="50" y="124" width="6" height="54" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".7"/><rect x="124" y="124" width="6" height="54" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".7"/><rect x="42" y="78" width="96" height="46" rx="3" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".6"/><path d="M36 78L90 48L144 78Z" fill="#c4783a"/><line x1="42" x2="138" y1="124" y2="124" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="2"/><text x="90" y="106" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">1 foot</text><rect x="210" y="72" width="6" height="106" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".7"/><rect x="284" y="72" width="6" height="106" fill="#5c6b78" fill-opacity=".7"/><rect x="202" y="26" width="96" height="46" rx="3" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".6"/><path d="M196 26L250 -4L304 26Z" fill="#c4783a"/><line x1="202" x2="298" y1="72" y2="72" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="2"/><text x="250" y="54" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="#1c2028">3 feet</text></svg><figcaption>Left, the old rule of 1 foot. Right, the 3 feet the county requires now in AE and VE zones in the unincorporated county. Our drawing.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our agents check the height rule for the lot's flood zone with the county or the city before you buy, because it changes the cost of the build. What a flood zone means for insurance is on <a href="/buyers/flood-insurance-after-the-election/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our page on flood insurance after the election</a> and <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">our coastal insurance guide</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="outlook"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Short and long term">What could happen to new home and land prices in Horry County, short and long term?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In the next year or two, we expect the pattern of the last five years to continue unless the national market turns. Over five years or more, Horry prices have fallen about 26% (2008 to 2012) and risen to about 2.4 times the low (2012 to 2026).</p><div class="g7-tab"><table><caption>What happened in Horry County before</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Period</th><th scope="col">What happened</th><th scope="col">Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">2006 to 2010</th><td><span class="l">What happened</span>Single-family permits fell from 6,484 to 1,388, about four in five fewer.</td><td><span class="l">Source</span><a href="https://www2.census.gov/econ/bps/County/co2025a.txt" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census permits</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2008 to 2012</th><td><span class="l">What happened</span>Prices fell about 26%, end of year to end of year.</td><td><span class="l">Source</span><a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/hpi/download/quarterly_datasets/hpi_at_metro.csv" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHFA index</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2012 to mid-2026</th><td><span class="l">What happened</span>Prices rose to about 2.4 times the 2012 low.</td><td><span class="l">Source</span><a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/hpi/download/quarterly_datasets/hpi_at_metro.csv" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHFA index</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">The year after each election, 2016 to 2024</th><td><span class="l">What happened</span>Prices rose every time, by 3% to 22%.</td><td><span class="l">Source</span><a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/hpi/download/quarterly_datasets/hpi_at_metro.csv" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHFA index</a></td></tr><tr><th scope="row">2021 to 2025</th><td><span class="l">What happened</span>Permits stayed between 6,356 and 7,269 a year.</td><td><span class="l">Source</span><a href="https://www2.census.gov/econ/bps/County/co2025a.txt" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census permits</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The next one to two years</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Local prices rose in the year after each of the last five elections, whichever party won. The latest yearly change, 0.8%, is inside the index's error of about 2%, so it reads as flat. If the national market holds, we expect permits to stay in the 2021 to 2025 range in the table. If it turns as it did from 2006 to 2010, plan for that slowdown, the first row of the table.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Five years and longer</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We have no verified past example of what a new interstate did to Horry land prices, so we make no estimate for <span class="nw">I-73</span>.</p><div class="g7-tab"><table><caption>Each leader's plan, under the same test</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Plan</th><th scope="col">Who must act</th><th scope="col">Past Horry example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Faster road building (Wilson)</th><td><span class="l">Who must act</span>The governor's office and the General Assembly</td><td><span class="l">Past Horry example</span>None in our data</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Faster home permits (Wilson)</th><td><span class="l">Who must act</span>The General Assembly, and county and city councils</td><td><span class="l">Past Horry example</span>None in our data</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Homebuilder tax cuts and a tariff change (Andrews)</th><td><span class="l">Who must act</span>Both houses of Congress and the president</td><td><span class="l">Past Horry example</span>None in our data</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Less red tape for energy projects (Andrews)</th><td><span class="l">Who must act</span>Both houses of Congress and the president</td><td><span class="l">Past Horry example</span>None in our data</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A pause on AI data centers (Andrews)</th><td><span class="l">Who must act</span>Both houses of Congress and the president</td><td><span class="l">Past Horry example</span>None in our data</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">With no past Horry example for any of these plans, we make no estimate for any of them.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want to buy a new construction home?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">See new homes across Horry County with an agent who knows the road plans.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Talk to a specialized agent</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="what-we-check"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="What we check">What does Chapter3 check before you buy land or a new home in Horry County?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our agents check four things for the lot you want, from the county's own records and the road plans:</p><ol class="g7-steps" aria-label="What we check for a lot or a new home"><li><strong>Permits around the lot</strong> We pull the building permits near it, so you see what is being built around it before you buy.</li><li><strong>The flood height</strong> We confirm the county's current floor-height rule for the lot's flood zone, and what it means for the build.</li><li><strong>Road plans</strong> We check the RIDE IV project list and the <span class="nw">I-73</span> route for work near the lot.</li><li><strong>The fees</strong> We ask the builder whether the impact fee is in the price.</li></ol></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="horry-county-growth-and-roads-questions"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Horry County growth and roads questions</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">When will I-73 be complete in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No date has been announced. SCDOT has not identified the money for most of the estimated 1.6 billion dollar cost, and work cannot begin until it does.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How much are Horry County impact fees?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A single-family home of about 1,500 square feet pays 1,615 dollars in total under the county's current schedule. A larger home or another type of building pays a different amount.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Was a road tax on the Horry County ballot in 2026?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. The 2026 Horry County ballot had no ballot question. Voters approved the RIDE IV sales tax in 2024, with 56.78 percent voting yes.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who can change impact fees in Horry County?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The county council, by ordinance with a positive majority, after a report on the effect on affordable housing. The state Development Impact Fee Act allows the fees.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is Horry County still building as many homes?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. The county's 2025 count was 6,418 single-family homes, after 7,269 in 2024, the most of any year from 2005 to 2025.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www2.census.gov/econ/bps/County/co2025a.txt" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census permits</a>, <a href="https://www.fhfa.gov/hpi/download/quarterly_datasets/hpi_at_metro.csv" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHFA index</a>, <a href="https://scvotes.gov/2026-general-election-results/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC election results</a>, <a href="https://www.wmbfnews.com/2026/11/04/election-results-whos-winning-grand-strand-pee-dee/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WMBF, Horry results</a>, <a href="https://www.wistv.com/2026/08/26/i-73-project-gains-renewed-attention-after-trump-pledge/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WIS, I-73</a>, <a href="https://www.wmbfnews.com/2026/02/10/scdot-makes-progress-right-of-way-acquisition-proposed-i-73/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WMBF, right of way</a>, <a href="https://www.wmbfnews.com/2026/07/21/impact-fee-schedule-change-now-effect-concerns-raised-over-homeownership/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">WMBF, impact fees</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/emergency-management/flooding/build-responsibly/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County, flood building rules</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/3rxjkrew/horry-county-resiliency-report-summary_07oct22.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County, Flood Resilience Master Plan summary</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/departments/stormwater/engineers/resources-tools/ordinances-manuals-and-reports/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County Code, Chapter 9</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t06c001.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">S.C. Code 6-1-930</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t06c029.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">S.C. Code 6-29-720</a>, <a href="https://drannieandrews.com/issue/transportation-infrastructure-that-works/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Andrews, infrastructure</a>, <a href="https://drannieandrews.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Web-file.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Andrews, plan</a>, <a href="https://www.wistv.com/2026/10/28/full-interview-alan-wilson-shares-priorities-governor-ahead-election-day/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wilson, WIS interview</a>, <a href="https://www.wmbfnews.com/2026/09/30/sc-governor-candidates-split-taxes-infrastructure-ahead-registration-deadlines/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wilson, WMBF</a>. Election results are unofficial. RIDE IV terms are from the ballot text. Not legal or investment advice.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">The Horry County courthouse and county office complex in Conway, the county seat. Photo: Pollinator, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-e/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/horry-county-government-complex-conway-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-e/horry-county-growth-roads-permits/horry-county-government-complex-conway-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":800,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"The Horry County courthouse and county office complex in Conway, the county seat","caption":"The Horry County courthouse and county office complex in Conway, the county seat.","description":"The Horry County courthouse and county office complex in Conway, two red brick buildings joined by a white entrance","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Pollinator"},"creditText":"Pollinator, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons","copyrightNotice":"© Pollinator, CC BY-SA 3.0","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Horry_County_Courthouse_and_county_office_complex,_Conway,_South_Carolina_(18_November_2006).jpg","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"Horry County courthouse and county office complex, Conway, South Carolina"}}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Buying land or a new home in Horry County?</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">Tell us where you want to build or buy. An agent helps you choose the lot or the home and buy it.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/">Talk to a specialized agent</a></div></section></main>
