-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I deduct Airbnb losses from my W-2 income?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "For a long-term rental, up to $25,000 of loss can reduce your salary income if you take part in the management decisions. The full amount applies while your income is $100,000 or less, and it shrinks above that. It does not apply to a short-term rental with average stays of seven days or less. There, the loss can reduce your salary only if you pass a material participation test."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the seven-day rule?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "If the average guest stay across the year is seven days or less, the property is not a rental activity for tax purposes. It is treated as a business. That removes the automatic passive label. You still need to pass a material participation test."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Do I need to be a real estate professional?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. That is a different rule with its own hour requirements. The seven-day route does not use it."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does a property manager rule me out?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "It makes the tests harder. Several tests compare your hours with everyone else who worked on the property. If the manager worked more hours than you, test 3 fails. If you do not need the loss against your salary, a manager is fine."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What records do I need?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "A calendar, an appointment book, or a written summary of your hours. Daily logs are not required. Start on the day you close."}}]}</script>
+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context": "https://schema.org", "@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "Can I deduct Airbnb losses from my W-2 income?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "For a long-term rental, up to $25,000 of loss can reduce your salary income if you take part in the management decisions. The full amount applies while your income is $100,000 or less. Above that it shrinks by 50 cents per dollar, and it is gone at $150,000. It does not apply to a short-term rental with average stays of seven days or less. There, the loss can reduce your salary only if you pass a material participation test."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What is the seven-day rule?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "If the average guest stay across the year is seven days or less, the property is not a rental activity for tax purposes. It is treated as a business. That removes the automatic passive label. You still need to pass a material participation test."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Do I need to be a real estate professional?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "No. That is a different rule with its own hour requirements. The seven-day route does not use it."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does a property manager rule me out?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "It makes the tests harder. Several tests compare your hours with everyone else who worked on the property. If the manager worked more hours than you, test 3 fails. If you do not need the loss against your salary, a manager is fine."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "What records do I need?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "A calendar, an appointment book, or a written summary of your hours. Daily logs are not required. Start on the day you close."}}]}</script>
