-<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","@id":"https://chapter3realty.com/sell/sell-my-condo/#faq","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"How long does it take to sell a condo in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The average condo took 137 days to sell as of June 2026, per the local REALTOR association, against 121 days for single-family homes. Condo supply sits at 7.6 months, which favors buyers, but pending condo sales were up 21.5 percent year over year, so priced-right units are moving."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the median Myrtle Beach condo price right now?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The area condo median was $244,270 in June 2026, up 2.0 percent from a year earlier, and condo sellers received 96.2 percent of list price on average. City-of-Myrtle-Beach condos specifically have been softer than the area number."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What do I legally have to disclose when selling a condo in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The South Carolina Residential Property Condition Disclosure Statement, including its owners-association addendum covering fees, assessments, and rental restrictions, delivered before the purchase contract is signed. If the unit takes vacation rentals, state law also requires written disclosure of every future booked rental period before the contract is ratified."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What happens to my rental bookings when I sell my condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Under South Carolina's Vacation Rental Act, the buyer takes title subject to bookings that begin within 90 days of the deed being recorded, so those guests keep their reservations. Bookings further out than 90 days do not bind the buyer unless the contract says so, and every future booked period must be disclosed in writing before ratification."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do I need a resale certificate to sell a condo in South Carolina?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. South Carolina has no statutory resale certificate or estoppel package like some states. Buyers rely on the recorded master deed and bylaws plus whatever documents the contract requires, and fee allocation for HOA paperwork is negotiated by checkbox in the standard SC contract."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why did my condo buyer's loan fall through?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Usually the building, not the buyer. Conventional lenders review the project: hotel-style rental operations, an underinsured master policy, low reserves, or litigation can each make the building ineligible mid-transaction. Knowing how your building reviews before you list, and having a lender who can finance it anyway, prevents the 30 to 45 days a doomed contract wastes."}}]}</script>
