"text-align:right;padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">$230,125 <span style="color:#b3554d;font-weight:600">-5.0%</span></td><td style="text-align:right;padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">$370,000</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Average days to an accepted offer</td><td style="text-align:right;padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">137</td><td style="text-align:right;padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">118</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Percent of list received</td><td style="text-align:right;padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">95.9%</td><td style="text-align:right;padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">97.3%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">Months of supply</td><td style="text-align:right;padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">7.5</td><td style="text-align:right;padding:.55rem .9rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule)">4.2</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:720px;margin-top:1.2rem;font-size:.9rem">Source: <a href="https://www.ccarsc.org/clientuploads/2026_Market_Stats/October_2026_Monthly_Market_Report.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">CCAR Monthly Indicators, September 2026</a>, current as of October 10, 2026. Monthly updates are on our <a href="/market-reports/july-2026/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">market report</a>.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The strategy</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Pricing a Myrtle Beach condo to its real buyer pool</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The type of building you own limits who can get a loan to buy your condo. A unit in a building that passes a lender's review can sell to any financed buyer. A unit in a hotel-style oceanfront tower usually cannot. FHA and VA buyers are almost never an option there, since only eight condo projects in all of Horry County were FHA-approved as of July 2026, and none were oceanfront. Regular conventional loans often fail the building review too. The real buyers for that tower are cash buyers, and DSCR or portfolio-loan buyers making large down payments. Unpaid assessments follow the unit, so request an estoppel statement: see <a href="/hoa/liens-and-foreclosure/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">liens and unpaid dues</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Across the country, about a third of recent home sales were all cash, mostly vacation-home buyers and investors. That is exactly who buys here. This is not bad news. It is useful information about which buyers to target. A condo priced and marketed to buyers who can close will not waste 30 to 45 days on a loan that was never going to fund, in a market that already takes 137 days.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">This is where we are different. Our preferred lender, <a href="/invest/condotel-financing/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">BrickWood Mortgage, finances condotel and non-warrantable buildings</a> at normal fees. When a buyer's agent asks how someone could finance your unit, we have an answer instead of a dead end. That widens your buyer pool in exactly the buildings where other listings lose buyers. Start by finding your building in our <a href="/invest/condo-buildings/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">oceanfront building directory</a>.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The strategy</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Pricing a Myrtle Beach condo to its real buyer pool</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The type of building you own limits who can get a loan to buy your condo. A unit in a building that passes a lender's review can sell to any financed buyer. A unit in a hotel-style oceanfront tower usually cannot. FHA and VA buyers are almost never an option there, since only eight condo projects in all of Horry County were FHA-approved as of July 2026, and none were oceanfront. Regular conventional loans often fail the building review too. The real buyers for that tower are cash buyers, and DSCR or portfolio-loan buyers making large down payments. Unpaid assessments follow the unit, so request an estoppel statement: see <a href="/hoa/liens-and-foreclosure/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">liens and unpaid dues</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Across the country, about a third of recent home sales were all cash, mostly vacation-home buyers and investors. That is exactly who buys here. This is not bad news. It is useful information about which buyers to target. A condo priced and marketed to buyers who can close will not waste 30 to 45 days on a loan that was never going to fund, in a market where condos already averaged 137 days to an accepted offer.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">This is where we are different. Our affiliated lender, BrickWood Mortgage, a separate company, <a href="/invest/condotel-financing/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">arranges condotel and non-warrantable loans</a> through wholesale lenders that accept these buildings. When a buyer's agent asks how someone could finance your unit, we have an answer instead of a dead end. That widens your buyer pool in exactly the buildings where other listings lose buyers. Start by finding your building in our <a href="/invest/condo-buildings/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">oceanfront building directory</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Chapter 3 Realty has a business relationship with BrickWood Mortgage. The full RESPA Affiliated Business Arrangement disclosure is in the footer of this page and <a href="/buyers/programs/#afba" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">here</a>. You are never required to use BrickWood, and you are free to shop lenders.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">A deal we saved</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">A Myrtle Beach condo deal saved by fixing the HOA master policy</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A buyer in the area went under contract on a condo. Partway through the loan, the problem surfaced: the association did not carry enough insurance. If a hurricane destroyed the building, the owners would have had to pay to rebuild it. That one issue made the whole building ineligible for financing, and it had nothing to do with the unit, the buyer, or the seller.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Instead of the deal collapsing, the HOA was called and put in touch with a new insurance agent, and the association changed its master policy for the whole building so the buyer could close. Along the way, the HOA gave the lender three different roof ages before one was finally confirmed, and its outside document company charged the buyer $500 just to hand over the paperwork the loan needed.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Those lessons apply to sellers too. Your building's insurance, reserves, and paperwork habits are part of your listing whether you like it or not. We check all of this before we list your home, because a problem like this can cost you money in negotiations if the buyer's agent finds it before we do.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The building review</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">How an HOA master policy can stop a condo sale</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A lender reviews the building as well as the buyer. An underinsured master policy is one of the things that can make a building fail that review partway through a loan, for reasons that have nothing to do with the unit, the buyer or the seller.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Those lessons apply to sellers too. Your building's insurance, reserves, and paperwork habits are part of your listing whether you like it or not. We check all of this before we list your home, because a problem like this can cost you money in negotiations if the buyer's agent finds it before we do.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The law</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What South Carolina law requires a condo seller to disclose</h2><ul style="list-style:none;display:grid;gap:.8rem;margin:1.2rem 0 0;padding:0;max-width:760px"><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.65"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Give the buyer the disclosure before they sign.</strong> South Carolina makes you hand the buyer a Residential Property Condition Disclosure Statement before the contract is signed. Since 2018 it includes an HOA section, where you list the fees, assessments, and rental rules. The law is <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c050.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">S.C. Code Title 27, Chapter 50</a>.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.65"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">List every future booking in writing before the contract is final.</strong> If the unit is a vacation rental, state law makes you disclose all upcoming bookings in writing before the contract is ratified. The buyer must honor any booking that starts within 90 days of closing, so those guests keep their reservations.</span></li><li style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.65"><span style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:700">&#10003;</span><span><strong style="color:var(--navy)">There is no resale certificate in South Carolina.</strong> Unlike Florida or Texas, the state has no required resale package. The buyer relies on the recorded master deed, the bylaws, the budget, and whatever the contract asks for. Who pays each HOA fee is just a checkbox in the standard state contract.</span></li></ul></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The paperwork</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Myrtle Beach condo HOA documents and third-party fees</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Many associations here hire outside companies to hold their records, and those companies charge for everything. In the deal above, the buyer paid $500 just for the documents the loan required, on the company's schedule, not the closing's. Estoppel letters, transfer fees, and capital contributions differ by community. A transfer fee is only legal if the building's documents allow it, and slow document companies are a common reason closings run late.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Two things protect a condo seller. First, find out on day one whether your association uses an outside document company and what it charges. Second, order the document package the week you list, not the week you go under contract. If you are also buying, check the same thing on the building you are buying into, because these fees apply to purchases and refinances alike.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The paperwork</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Myrtle Beach condo HOA documents and third-party fees</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Estoppel letters, transfer fees, and capital contributions differ by community. A transfer fee is only legal if the building's documents allow it, and slow document companies are a common reason closings run late.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Two things protect a condo seller. First, find out on day one whether your association uses an outside document company and what it charges. Second, order the document package the week you list, not the week you go under contract. If you are also buying, check the same thing on the building you are buying into, because these fees apply to purchases and refinances alike.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Bookings and furniture</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Selling a rental condo with future bookings</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A full booking calendar usually helps you, it does not hurt. The rental history and upcoming bookings prove the unit's income to an investor buyer, which is what a <a href="/invest/strategies/dscr-loans/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">DSCR loan</a> is based on, and our <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Airbnb income report</a> shows what buyers will compare your numbers to. As a rule, <a href="/sell/rental-property/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">deposits for stays after closing pass to the buyer at closing</a>, rental income is split at the closing date, and oceanfront units are sold furnished with an inventory list. Your contract controls all three, so put them in writing rather than assume them.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Showings work around guests. In peak season, a booked week is often shown with photos, video, and a floor plan instead of in person. That matters less than it sounds, because most buyers for these units live out of state and shop online anyway. That is why our <a href="/sell/out-of-state-buyers/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">out-of-state marketing</a> matters more for condos than for any house.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--navy)"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;padding:3rem 1.5rem"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.9rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:.8rem">Get a condo pricing analysis built on your building.</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.6);max-width:600px;margin:0 auto 1.6rem;line-height:1.7">We price from the building out: who can buy it, how they can finance it through BrickWood Mortgage, what the fees and insurance are, and then the comps. In a market with this much supply, that order is what sells the unit.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact/">Price my condo</a><a href="/sell/" style="font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:600;font-size:.9rem;color:var(--brass-2);text-decoration:none;align-self:center">How we sell →</a></div></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--navy)"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;padding:3rem 1.5rem"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.9rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:.8rem">Get a condo pricing analysis built on your building.</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.6);max-width:600px;margin:0 auto 1.6rem;line-height:1.7">We price from the building out: who can buy it, how they can finance it, including through BrickWood Mortgage, our affiliated lender, what the fees and insurance are, and then the comps. In a market with this much supply, that order is what sells the unit.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.75rem;justify-content:center;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/contact/">Price my condo</a><a href="/sell/" style="font-family:var(--sans);font-weight:600;font-size:.9rem;color:var(--brass-2);text-decoration:none;align-self:center">How we sell →</a></div></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Selling a Myrtle Beach condo FAQ</h2><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">How long does it take to sell a condo in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">The average condo took 137 days to sell as of June 2026, per the local REALTOR association, against 121 days for single-family homes. Condo supply sits at 7.6 months, which favors buyers, but pending condo sales were up 21.5 percent year over year, so priced-right units are moving.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What is the median Myrtle Beach condo price right now?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">The area condo median was $244,270 in June 2026, up 2.0 percent from a year earlier, and condo sellers received 96.2 percent of list price on average. City-of-Myrtle-Beach condos specifically have been softer than the area number.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What do I legally have to disclose when selling a condo in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">The South Carolina Residential Property Condition Disclosure Statement, including its owners-association addendum covering fees, assessments, and rental restrictions, delivered before the purchase contract is signed. If the unit takes vacation rentals, state law also requires written disclosure of every future booked rental period before the contract is ratified.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">What happens to my rental bookings when I sell my condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Under South Carolina's Vacation Rental Act, the buyer takes title subject to bookings that begin within 90 days of the deed being recorded, so those guests keep their reservations. Bookings further out than 90 days do not bind the buyer unless the contract says so, and every future booked period must be disclosed in writing before ratification.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Do I need a resale certificate to sell a condo in South Carolina?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">No. South Carolina has no statutory resale certificate or estoppel package like some states. Buyers rely on the recorded master deed and bylaws plus whatever documents the contract requires, and fee allocation for HOA paperwork is negotiated by checkbox in the standard SC contract.</p></div><div style="border-top:1px solid var(--rule);padding:1.4rem 0"><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.2rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:.5rem">Why did my condo buyer's loan fall through?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:680px">Usually the building, not the buyer. Conventional lenders review the project: hotel-style rental operations, an underinsured master policy, low reserves, or litigation can each make the building ineligible mid-transaction. Knowing how your building reviews before you list, and having a lender who can finance it anyway, prevents the 30 to 45 days a doomed contract wastes.</p></div></div></section>
