-<div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/submarkets/">Neighborhoods</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Carolina Forest</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">Community guide &middot; verified July 2026</p><h1 class="detail-h1">Carolina Forest, SC real estate:<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">home prices, eleven subdivisions, no city tax.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; Published July 20, 2026</p><p class="detail-sub">Carolina Forest is the Myrtle Beach area&#39;s biggest master-planned community: 26,000+ residents, eleven subdivisions, no city tax, and no wind-pool insurance surcharge.</p></div></div>
+<div class="detail-hero bg-grid"><div class="wrap"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/submarkets/">Neighborhoods</a><span>/</span><span style="color:var(--muted)">Carolina Forest</span></div><p class="eyebrow" style="margin-bottom:1rem">Community guide</p><style>main#main .detail-h1 em{display:block;font-size:clamp(1.3rem,2.5vw,2.25rem);line-height:1.25;margin-top:.55rem;text-wrap:balance}</style><h1 class="detail-h1">Carolina Forest, SC real estate:<br/><em style="font-style:italic;color:var(--brass)">home prices, eleven subdivisions, no city tax.</em></h1><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem;margin-bottom:1rem">By <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Devin Day</strong>, Operations Officer &middot; Reviewed by <strong style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600">Tim Nash</strong>, Broker-in-Charge &middot; Published July 20, 2026</p><p class="detail-sub">Carolina Forest is the Myrtle Beach area&#39;s biggest master-planned community: 26,000+ residents, eleven subdivisions, no city tax, and no wind-pool insurance surcharge.</p></div></div>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The direct answer</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">What Carolina Forest is, and why relocating buyers choose it</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">Carolina Forest is unincorporated Horry County between US 501 and International Drive, master-planned by International Paper in the late 1990s on part of its old 30,000-acre timber tract. It is not a city and pays no city taxes; the mailing address says Myrtle Beach, ZIP 29579, and the beach is a 10-to-15-minute drive. The 2020 Census counted 23,342 residents; the latest Census estimate puts it near 26,263, and 14.5 percent of residents moved in within the past year, roughly ten points above the state's migration rate. </p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">The draw is a combination of four things: twenty years of continuous <a href="/buyers/new-construction/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">new construction</a>, single-family prices well below beach product, no municipal millage, and simpler insurance. Insurance is standard homeowner coverage that still includes wind, because most of Carolina Forest is outside the state wind pool's territory. Most of our <a href="/buyers/relocating/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">relocating clients</a> tour it first, and many stop looking after.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The market</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Carolina Forest home prices in 2026</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">The two best reads, each with its own lens: Redfin's Carolina Forest page showed a median sale price of $418,000, up 7.7 percent year over year, with homes going under contract in about 105 days as of mid-2026. SC Realtors data for ZIP 29579 showed a $464,710 single-family median in December 2025, up from $426,500 a year earlier, on 129 days of market time. Condos along the corridor, the area's entry tier, ran a median near $215,500, up about 17 percent.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">Context makes those numbers make sense: the Horry County median sits near $329,000 and the City of Myrtle Beach near $266,000, but both are dragged down by condo-heavy stock. Carolina Forest trades at a premium to the county because it is almost entirely single-family product in amenity communities, and local press has described it as a seller's market even while beach condos favor buyers. Trailing twelve months, roughly 1,330 homes changed hands here. Current numbers for any specific community: ask, and see the <a href="/market-reports/july-2026/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">July 2026 market report</a> for the region-wide picture.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The market</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Carolina Forest home prices</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">County deed records give the prices. In the year to September 2026, a house in Carolina Forest resold for about $440,000 at the middle, on 405 sales. Half sold between about $325,000 and $600,000. A condo resold for about $172,000 at the middle, on 91 sales, and a townhouse for about $230,000, on 34. These are resales in the Census-designated place of Carolina Forest; a builder's first sale is not counted.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">Most homes sold here are houses: 405 of the 530 house, condo and townhouse resales. Prices for one community, or one street, are a different number: ask us, and see the <a href="/market-reports/july-2026/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">July 2026 market report</a> for the region.</p></div></section>
 
