 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Schools</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Carolina Forest schools and attendance zones</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">The attendance area belongs to Horry County Schools: Carolina Forest High School, Ten Oaks and Ocean Bay middle schools, and five elementary schools. Carolina Forest High enrolls about 3,181 students, the fifth-largest public high school in South Carolina, ranked 33rd in the state by U.S. News, with 80 percent math and 91 percent reading proficiency against state averages of 43 and 52.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">The growth showed up in the classrooms first: by 2024, Carolina Forest Elementary ran at 136 percent of capacity, Ocean Bay at 144, and River Oaks at 166. The district's answer opened in August 2025: Ten Oaks Elementary ($62.3 million) and Pine Island Elementary ($65.7 million), 1,200 seats each, with 1,920 students reassigned to fill them. Which school serves which subdivision is set by the district's attendance maps, and lines just moved, so we check the <a href="https://www.horrycountyschools.net/newsroom/2024-2025-news-pages/cfnewschools" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--brass);text-decoration:none">current district maps</a> on every showing rather than trusting what a home is advertised as.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">Districtwide, Horry County Schools posted 38 of 57 schools rated Excellent or Good on the 2025 state report cards, with none Unsatisfactory, and an 89.6 percent graduation rate.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Costs and risk</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Carolina Forest property taxes, flood zones, and wind insurance</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Taxes.</strong> Carolina Forest's 2026 levy totals about 201.0 mills: county base 52.1, schools 119.1, fire district 21.7, solid waste 8.1, plus an $89 stormwater fee and a $50 per-vehicle road fee. There is no city millage; Myrtle Beach city residents pay 83.4 additional mills for comparable houses. Owner-occupants at the 4 percent rate are also exempt from the 109.1 school-operating mills, which is most of the bill. Exact numbers for any price: our <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Horry County property tax calculator</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Flood.</strong> Much of the area maps outside FEMA high-risk zones, with Zone AE pockets near ponds and wetlands, so we pull the parcel-level map, in effect since December 16, 2021, on every home. A pond view and a flood requirement are sometimes the same lot; our <a href="/buyers/undisclosed-flooding/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">flood history guide</a> covers how we check what maps do not show.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Wind.</strong> Around Myrtle Beach, the state wind pool's territory reaches inland to Carolina Bays Parkway and River Oaks Drive. Most of Carolina Forest is inland of that line, outside the territory, so wind coverage stays inside a standard homeowner policy instead of a separate wind-pool policy. Homes between those roads and the Intracoastal Waterway are inside it. Against a beach address, that is a real annual saving; the full comparison is in our <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">coastal insurance guide</a>.</p></div></section>
 
