 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Growth</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Carolina Forest growth: roads, schools, and new development</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px;margin-bottom:1rem">The two long-running road complaints are resolved: the $54.7 million Carolina Forest Boulevard widening is complete, four lanes with a multi-use path, and the Postal Way extension is open. The RIDE 4 penny tax that took effect May 1, 2025 funds the next round, including an SC 31 interchange at Augusta Plantation Drive. County planners have also approved a 1,154-unit mixed-use village concept for the area, apartments over retail in the style of Market Common; its construction timing is not yet set.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:760px">The area's defining 2025 event was the Covington Drive Fire: 2,059 acres over 95 days, the longest-burning wildfire in South Carolina history, which forced evacuations in eight neighborhoods and destroyed zero homes. It reshaped how this market thinks about defensible space and insurance, and it is part of every honest conversation we have about buying along the preserve edges.</p></div></section>
 
